31 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 29 0 R
/E (Recent video world models have demonstrated strong capabilities in long-horizon, camera-controllable scene generation, creating new possibilities for embodied AI, simulation, and interactive environments. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
32 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 29 0 R
/E (However, generating visually plausible videos does not necessarily imply that the model maintains a coherent underlying 3D world. During extended camera motion, viewpoint changes, occlusion, or revisits, current models may exhibit geometric drift, scale changes, layout resets, object disappearance or duplication, and inconsistent scene reconstruction.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
33 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 29 0 R
/E (Existing benchmarks have increasingly incorporated pose estimation, depth consistency, reprojection, reconstruction, and revisit-based evaluation. Nevertheless, these approaches often evaluate different aspects of consistency in isolation and may conflate camera-control failure with failures of world persistence. More importantly, under single-frame initialization, previously unseen regions are not uniquely determined by the input image, making direct comparison with hidden ground truth potentially inappropriate.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
34 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 29 0 R
/E (This motivates the study of evidence-conditioned 3D persistence: whether a generated world remains consistent with the observable input evidence, can be explained by a stable 3D structure, and preserves both observed content and model-generated completions after departure and revisit. WorldAnchor addresses this gap through interactive viewpoint loops and observability-aware evaluation of long-horizon world consistency.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
